            <td data-label="Why">{item.why}</td>
            <td data-label="Who pays, and the first sales step">{item.who}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Ratings follow our scalability rule: a route is Scalable when skill, proof and sales sense can grow income into consulting or a business of your own. Crowded fields and ladders of exams and ranks are marked Not so scalable. The sales step is a basic starting point and promises no income.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Two-stage competition to plan for:</strong> medicine (NEET, then postgraduate seats), chartered accountancy (three exam levels plus articleship) and a top MBA (an entrance exam, then campus placement) each ask you to win twice. Clearing the first filter does not clear the second, so keep a backup skill that pays if the plan slips.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="small-part">Why the degree is a small part of income growth</h2>

  <figure class="owner-view">
    <p class="owner-view__label">Our view</p>
    <p>
      This is our editorial view, not a study result. We think the college and the degree explain a small share of how far your income grows, and that three other things explain more.
    </p>
  </figure>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">Skills</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">Core skill, multiplier skill, proof</div>
      <p>One skill the market pays for, one that multiplies it (communication in English, data, a second language, AI use with checking), and one finished piece of work that others can see. A pile of certificates is not a skill portfolio.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">Sales layer</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">Who pays and why they trust you</div>
      <p>Know who pays, say what you do in two sentences, show proof, follow up, ask for referrals, and learn to hold one honest price conversation. One number to watch: how many conversations turn into a paid yes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">Ownership</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">Own part of what you build</div>
      <p>A salary is a tool to save and learn. Larger income usually comes from owning a practice, a studio, a product, a client list or a share in a business. Not everyone needs this, but it is the layer a pay band cannot give you.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">The 3 Gates: pass these before you spend money on another credential.</p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      {gateItems.map((item) => (
        <div class="gate">
          <span class="gate__num">{item.name}</span>
          <span class="gate__name">{item.title}</span>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Degrees still matter where the law needs them. Medicine, nursing, law and chartered accountancy need the formal credential, and some top institutes give real access to peers, labs and recruiters. The problem is degree-only thinking, where a person spends heavily on a credential and builds nothing beside it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Information is now free or cheap on YouTube, official documentation and reputable low-cost courses. Judge each course by its instructor, update date, exercises and whether it makes you build something.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and your degree</h2>

  <p>
    AI changes the task mix inside a degree before it changes the degree. Ask which parts of your work become easier and which human layers still earn the money.
  </p>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    {aiRows.map((item) => (
      <div class="work-style-card">
        <div class="work-style-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="work-style-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Start now by using AI to explain, draft and check your own work, and verify every number from an official page. Later, as tools mature, the gain goes to people who pair domain knowledge with a clear workflow and can show the result. No multiplier is guaranteed, and tool skill without domain depth does not raise pay.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choosing">If you are choosing, or already hold, a degree</h2>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">If you are choosing</div>
      <ul>
        <li>As a strict heuristic, keep the college fee near 10% of the whole education budget. On Rs 10 lakh, that is about Rs 1 lakh for college and Rs 9 lakh for living security, tools, projects and later learning.</li>
        <li>Spending above roughly 20% needs course-specific evidence, such as a legal requirement or a published placed share and median.</li>
        <li>Check the work style too. Some skills can be sold remotely from a lower-cost city, while others need a hospital, a site or a court.</li>
        <li>Avoid an education loan unless the credential is legally required. If a degree needs a loan to pay back from first-year pay, run the numbers first.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">If you already hold one</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The degree is done. The question is whether the next stretch builds proof or only drift.</li>
        <li>Run a short test: one skill, one finished sample, and three conversations with people two to five years ahead of you, for as long as it genuinely takes. Some people need a few weeks, others a couple of months.</li>
        <li>For the broad BA, BSc and BCom group, the flattest slope in the data, a skill added early matters most. Data analysis, sales, design or writing sell directly and are stronger moves than a second general degree.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Expensive wrong turns</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Paying for a prestige seat because a brochure quoted a package.</li>
      <li>Taking a loan for a degree whose typical start is smaller than the yearly repayment.</li>
      <li>Collecting certificates instead of one finished project.</li>
      <li>Treating a salary list as a plan.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Pick the two degrees or routes you are weighing. For each one, find the typical start, the rupees added by year three and the share who reach a high band, from a named sample with a size. Then ask three people working in the role what they earned in year one and year three.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want a structured plan around your budget, marks and goals, choose <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">continuous career guidance</a>. It helps you make the decisions that unlock high income opportunities, by building a high-value skill portfolio around your degree so your income compounds toward earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

